sentences.l:
and they are grievous
for mortal men, at least, to be tamed and to be driven,
for any other than Achilles, whom an immortal mother bore.

Hector, for his part, among those, as many as are counsel-bearers,
is taking counsels beside the tomb of divine Ilus,
away from the din;

As many as are the Trojans’ hearths of fire, for whom there is need,
they are awake and bid to keep watch
one another;

For if now we release you, or let you go,
surely also later you will come to the swift ships of the Achaeans,
either to spy them out, or head-on to fight;

He spoke, and the one was about him by the chin with a thick hand
having touched to beg, but the other the neck in the middle struck
with a sword, darting, and both tendons cut off;

And they were sleeping, worn out with weariness, and their gear,
fine, beside them on the ground lay, well in due order,
in three rows;
